---
/* PUBLISHING YOUR OWN ARCHIVE ON THE TREE CANVAS.
 *
 * Somebody outside the eight family archives, with their own research, can draw it on the Tree Canvas in two ways. The first (drop a tree.json) is built and
 * is described here as what it does today; the second (an archive the canvas knows by name) is a decision the Atlas makes archive by archive, and is described
 * as that. The file format below is read off the reader that runs: site/public/roots/js/treejson.js, which is also what scripts/test-tree-json.mjs checks.
 *
 * WHAT IS NOT PROMISED: a box where anyone types a web address (it needs the page's security policy opened to every site), and any check of what a file says
 * about its own people beyond the shape and the living rule.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../../lib/url.js";
---
<Base
  title="Publishing your own archive on the Tree Canvas"
  eyebrow="Tree Canvas · for researchers who keep their own family history"
  dek="Draw your own research on the canvas with its page links and correction marks, from one file, without uploading it and without asking anyone.">

  <div class="k-hi">
    <p><b>Two ways, and only the first is open to everyone.</b> Drop a <code>tree.json</code> on the canvas and it draws your tree, read in your browser and never uploaded.
    Or ask for your site to be listed, so the canvas knows it by name, which is a decision the Atlas makes for each archive.</p>
    <p><a href={u("/tree-canvas/")}>Open the Tree Canvas</a> · <a href={u("/tree-canvas/example-tree.json")} download>Download an invented example file</a> to try it now.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="k-h" id="route1">Route 1: drop a tree.json <span class="k-count">open to anyone · available now</span></h2>
  <p class="k-lede">If you build a Roots &amp; Routes tree for your own site, it already makes a <code>tree.json</code>. Open the folder button on the canvas, or drag the file onto the page, as you would a
    GEDCOM. The canvas tells the two apart by what is inside, not by the file name.</p>
  <ul class="k-steps">
    <li><b>Nothing is uploaded and nobody has to approve it.</b> The file is read in your browser. The only requests the page makes are for the Atlas's own public files for the places your file names, the same ones a GEDCOM needs.</li>
    <li><b>What you get that a GEDCOM cannot give.</b> A link from each person to their own page on your site, a mark where you have recorded corrections or notes, full dates as you wrote them, the route a person travelled, and your sources. All of it comes from the file.</li>
    <li><b>It is labelled as yours.</b> It appears under the title in your file's <code>meta.title</code>, never as one of the Atlas's archives, and the canvas does not check what it says. You can still set it beside a family archive and compare.</li>
    <li><b>Anyone who may be alive is left as a name and a family.</b> This is applied to every file before anything is drawn, whatever the file says: a person marked <code>living</code>, or with no death year (or only an estimated one) and born after 1920, keeps their name and their family links, and loses dates, places, page link, sources and occupation. A careless export cannot put a living person's details on the canvas. The canvas says how many it did this to.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="k-h" id="format">The file format <span class="k-count">what the reader takes, and nothing more</span></h2>
  <p class="k-lede">The top level is an object with <code>meta</code> and <code>people</code> (a list), and optionally <code>places</code>. Each person is an object. Anything not in the table is ignored. Text is cut to a safe length, years must be whole numbers from 1000 to 2100, and a link to someone who is not in the file is dropped and counted.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table class="k-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Needed</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>A unique, stable text id. A repeated id is skipped.</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>sex</code></td><td><code>m</code>, <code>f</code> or <code>u</code></td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>given</code>, <code>sur</code></td><td>Given names, surname</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>by</code>, <code>dy</code></td><td>Birth year, death year (numbers). </td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bye</code>, <code>dye</code></td><td><code>true</code> when that year is an estimate. An estimated death year does not release someone born after 1920 from the living rule.</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bd</code>, <code>dd</code></td><td>Full date, such as <code>1851-03-02</code> or <code>2 Mar 1851</code></td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bp</code>, <code>dp</code></td><td>Birth place, death place, as text (see Places below)</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>father</code>, <code>mother</code></td><td>Ids of the parents</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>spouse</code>, <code>kids</code></td><td>Lists of ids. Where a child names a parent, or a parent lists a child, the other side is filled in.</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>occ</code>, <code>my</code></td><td>Occupation; marriage year</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>route</code></td><td>Places lived, in order: <code>[place, year, estimated]</code>, up to 40</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>page</code></td><td>The person's own page, a path on your site such as <code>/people/ivan/</code>. It is used only when <code>meta.site</code> names your site.</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cx</code></td><td>How many corrections and notes you have recorded about them (a count). It links to their <code>page</code>.</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>srcs</code></td><td>Sources: <code>{"{ t: title, u: https link }"}</code>, up to 20. Links that are not https are dropped.</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>surAlso</code></td><td>Other spellings of the surname recorded for them</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>living</code></td><td><code>true</code> to show the person as a name and family only</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>meta.title</code></td><td>The name the canvas shows for your tree</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>meta.site</code></td><td>Your site's address, https only, for example <code>https://family.example.org</code>. Page links and corrections links are built on it.</td><td>Needed for page links</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>

  <h2 class="k-h" id="places">Places <span class="k-count">how a name becomes a flag and a country</span></h2>
  <p class="k-lede">A place is text. Written with its country (<code>Rijeka, Croatia</code>), it is looked up in the Atlas's own place files, and the canvas draws the flag in force there in the year of the event, as it does for a GEDCOM.
    A bare name such as <code>Mascali</code> or <code>Melbourne</code> could be anywhere, so a file may also bring the places it has already placed, as Roots &amp; Routes writes them: a list <code>places</code> (or <code>atlas.places</code>) of
    <code>{"{ n, lat, lon, cn }"}</code>, the name exactly as used in the people, its coordinates, and its country (<code>Italy</code> or <code>IT</code>). Only those four are read. The flag, the rulers and the eras are still looked up in the Atlas, so
    nothing drawn comes from your file. A place that cannot be found stays as the text you wrote, with no flag, and the person is drawn without a map position.</p>

  <h2 class="k-h" id="route2">Route 2: a listed archive <span class="k-count">by request · one decision each time</span></h2>
  <p class="k-lede">An archive on the list opens by name from the canvas, with its own colour, page links and corrections marks, and can be compared with a GEDCOM like the eight family archives. It is the Atlas vouching for the site, so each one is a decision.</p>
  <ul class="k-steps">
    <li><b>An entry</b> is a host, a name and a colour in an allow-list, plus a change to the page's security policy so the canvas may connect to that host.</li>
    <li><b>Before one is added,</b> the site must publish a <code>/roots/</code> page in the same format, must leave out living people, and must keep its data public and stable. The Atlas can check the first by reading the file; the other two are the owner's word.</li>
    <li><b>Not offered for a listed archive:</b> a site cannot become a listed archive by typing its address. What is offered is Route 1 from a link: the first screen of the Tree Canvas has a box for the https address of a public GEDCOM or tree.json (a link of the form <code>/tree-canvas/?url=…</code> only fills that box; nothing is fetched until you press Open link). The page around the canvas fetches the file in your browser, never the canvas itself, and the security policy of that one page now lets it read an https address; the canvas and the rest of the site are unchanged. The limits: https only; a public website (no sign-in details, port, bare IP address or local name); the site must allow other websites to read the file (an <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> header, which GitHub's raw files and GitHub Pages send); 100 MB at most; nothing passes through Record Atlas; and the site it came from is named when the canvas opens. The file is treated exactly like one you opened yourself: labelled as your file, with the living rule applied.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="k-h" id="compare">The two side by side</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table class="k-table">
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Route 1: tree.json</th><th>Route 2: listed archive</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Who decides</td><td>You</td><td>The Atlas, per archive</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Privacy</td><td>The file stays in your browser</td><td>The canvas fetches your public <code>/roots/</code> page</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Page links and corrections</td><td>Yes, if the file carries them and names your site</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Appears as</td><td>Labelled as your file</td><td>Named, with its own colour</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Security policy</td><td>Unchanged for a file you drop; the opening page may read any https link you paste</td><td>One host added per archive</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Status</td><td>Available</td><td>Not offered yet</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>

  <div class="k-foot">
    <p><a href={u("/tree-canvas/")}>Open the Tree Canvas</a> · <a href={u("/connect/")}>Connect to the Atlas</a> · <a href={u("/about/")}>About</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>
